the federal minimum wage hasn't gone up  since 2009 it's seven dollars and  twenty-five cents an hour but some  states and cities have raised wages  locally this week Los Angeles followed  in Seattle and San Francisco's footsteps  raising its minimum wage to fifteen  dollars an hour that'll happen by 2020  corporations are also taking this matter  into their own hands a few months ago  gap announced that 65,000 of its workers  will see their pay go up and it's not  the only retailer to have done this big  box stores are also paying their workers  a bit more Walmart announced that all  new workers will make at least nine  dollars an hour in 2015 and that's going  to go up to ten dollars an hour next  year we learned this week that that  dragged down Walmart's earnings last  quarter but executives say they've done  the cost-benefit analysis this is  actually going to help Walmart in the  long run you see there are two reasons  for that one is companies are having to  work harder to attract workers and to  keep workers because the economy has  gotten better the other is they're  facing mounting pressure a few months  ago there was a big campaign to raise  wages in the fast food sector to fifteen  dollars an hour well starting on July  first the average hourly wage for new  worker McDonald's will be nine dollars  and ninety cents there is a catch to  that however that's only going to happen  at McDonald's restaurants that are owned  by mcdonald's and most mcdonald's are  owned by franchisees so this is really  going to affect workers at about ten  percent of mcdonald's locations in the  u.s. a single person is considered poor  if he makes eleven thousand seven  hundred dollars or less a family of four  is considered below the poverty line at  household income is twenty four thousand  two hundred fifty dollars or less so  what would someone living below the  poverty line have to make to get out of  poverty well there's a lot of complexity  to that question geography is one thing  size of household but according to Ken  Jacobs who's a labor economist at the  University of California Berkeley a  single mother with one kid working 30  hours a week we need to make thirteen  dollars and twelve cents an hour
 

